# Adversarial referee report

**LIN’s core argument survives; the advertised factoring optimality does not follow.** I found a false HW corollary, a logarithmic error in LIN′’s proposed proof, and material literature and scope errors. “FATAL” below concerns the advertised conclusion, not a counterexample to LIN itself.

## 1. Theorem LIN

**1. MINOR — Lemma 1 is essentially correct, with a missing phase.**

For every odd positive \(n\), including nonsquarefree \(n\),
\[
\chi_{-4m}(n)=(-4m/n)=(-m/n).
\]

For odd nonsquare \(r\), the Jacobi character modulo \(r\) is nonprincipal: some prime exponent is odd, and CRT supplies a unit with character \(-1\). Restricting \(m\equiv1\pmod4\) does not change this because \((4,r)=1\). Square \(r=s^2\) gives the stated coprimality indicator and density.

Poisson needs the translation phase \(e(\pm hQ/(4r))\), possibly absorbed into \(\tau_h\). Its omission does not affect the bounds. Arbitrarily strong power savings follow by increasing the Fourier-decay exponent, absorbing polynomial \(B\).

**Fix:** insert or explicitly absorb the phase, and distinguish the requested saving exponent from the Fourier-decay exponent.

**2. MINOR — The test-set explanation is wrong; its conclusion is repairable.**

Removing the \(K\) smallest classes need not minimize the weighted sum: \(\varphi(k)/k^3\) is not monotone. A positive mean alone also does not establish that the constant \(8\) suffices.

Instead use
\[
\sum_{\substack{k\le x\\k\text{ odd squarefree}}}\frac{\varphi(k)}k
\sim ax,\qquad
a=\frac12\prod_{\ell>2}(1-2/\ell^2+1/\ell^3)>\frac14.
\]
Deleting \(K\) terms removes at most \(K\) from this sum. Multiplying the remainder by \((8K)^{-2}\) proves the required \(c/K\). Handle bounded \(K\) separately.

**Fix:** replace the deletion argument by this calculation.

**3. MINOR — Lemma 2 needs explicit bookkeeping, but its bound is valid.**

Heath–Brown’s Theorem 1 is quoted correctly: **both variables are positive odd squarefree**, and the bound contains
\[
(MN)^\eta(M+N)\sum|a_n|^2.
\]
[Original paper, pp. 237–238](https://matwbn.icm.edu.pl/ksiazki/aa/aa72/aa7234.pdf).

The square-part identity is correct. For fixed \(s,t\), put \(1_{(n_1,t)=1}\) into the coefficients, retain \(1_{(t,s)=1}\), and discard the **outer** restriction \((m_1,s)=1\) only after taking absolute squares. Since \(m_1\equiv1\pmod4\), reciprocity leaves the coefficient twist \((-1/n_1)\). Factor \(s^{-2}\) outside before applying HB.

The resulting block norm satisfies
\[
\|B_Y\|_2
\ll Q^{o(1)}
\sum_{s\le\sqrt{2Y}}s^{-2}(Qs^2/Y+\sqrt Q)^{1/2}
\ll Q^{o(1)}
\left(\sqrt{Q/Y}\log(2Y)+Q^{1/4}\right).
\]
Squaring and summing blocks yields the claimed bound, choosing HB’s exponent sufficiently small. The \(\sqrt Q\) comes from summing the \(N\)-term over \(t\). The far tail is negligible.

**Fix:** write this derivation explicitly.

**4. MINOR — The frame and final balance check out.**

Distinct squarefree tests have nonsquare product; their normalized Gram matrix has operator norm \(1+o(1)\). The product bounds fit Lemma 1 for sufficiently large \(Q\).

The tail correlation is \(O(Q^{3/4+\varepsilon/4})\), while the retained diagonal is
\[
\gg Q^{3/4+\varepsilon}/\log\log Q.
\]
Thus the mean-square bound and existence of an \(m\) with error \(\gg K^{-1/2}\) follow. The order class-number formula also holds for nonfundamental \(-4m<-4\).

**Fix:** retain LIN after these proof repairs; do not extend its range to the AFE length.

**5. MAJOR — The model is meaningful but substantially narrower than the verdict.**

It requires common, nonadaptive support \(S\), \(PK\le Q^{1-\varepsilon}\), and uniform smoothness at scale \(Q\).

Fixed-cutoff partial sums, smooth truncations, and short AFE sub-selections fit. Euler products do not fit LIN at their prime-operation cost. Booker’s complete algorithm includes algebraic work outside the model.

The implemented random sampler has \(P\asymp Q\) and is excluded. Shorter random samplers can fit: PV permits cutoff roughly \(\sqrt{QK}\) times logarithms at accuracy \(K^{-1/2}\).

**Fix:** describe a restricted quadrature theorem, with coverage stated algorithm by algorithm. It is neither vacuous nor a general analytic-computation lower bound.

## 2. LIN′, semiprimes, and ARCH

**6. MAJOR — LIN′’s prime-test calculation loses an extra logarithm.**

After excluding the first \(K\) primes, tests reach \(x\asymp K\log K\), and
\[
\sum_{\ell>x}\ell^{-2}
\asymp\frac1{x\log x}
\asymp\frac1{K(\log K)^2}.
\]
The proposed frame argument therefore gives
\[
\frac1{\sqrt K\log K},
\]
not \(1/\sqrt{K\log K}\). The exact odd-power coefficient is \(\operatorname{atanh}(1/\ell)\), with the same scale.

The stronger **existential** claim might have another proof, but this argument does not establish it.

**Fix:** weaken the rate, prove the logarithmic tail estimate, state support restrictions, and include the GRH \(1/\log\log Q\) loss when converting to absolute \(L\)-error. This correction does not change the power \(1/5\).

**7. MAJOR — The semiprime sketch is plausible, not established.**

GRH prime-character bounds plausibly give \(Q^{1/2+o(1)}\) after separation. But the family has mass \(\asymp Q/(\log Q)^2\), not \(Q\).

Missing details include the balanced region, congruence condition, ordering and diagonal removal, imprimitive characters, smooth-weight norms, and uniform conductor dependence. Both Gram bounds and target correlations need the correct normalization.

Restricting HB’s nonnegative outer sum to squarefree semiprimes is legitimate; this does not automatically establish the other estimates.

**Fix:** provide the complete GRH proof with logarithmic margins in \(BK^2\ll\sqrt Q\).

**8. FATAL — ARCH does not establish factoring optimality as advertised.**

LIN produces a difficult integer \(m\), potentially nonsquarefree, rather than a balanced semiprime. Its factoring application depends on finding 7.

ARCH also drops the position hypothesis: small \(K\) does not imply \(PK\le Q^{1-\varepsilon}\). Finally, BSGS’s \(O(\sqrt\Delta)\) upper bound is not an \(\Omega(\sqrt\Delta)\) lower bound for every finishing algorithm.

The optimization
\[
\min_K\{K+(Q/K)^{1/4}\}\asymp Q^{1/5}
\]
is correct **if that finishing cost is imposed**.

For randomized algorithms, seedwise mean-square error additionally does not imply expected finishing cost or bounded-error complexity; rare large errors can dominate the second moment.

**Fix:** state a deterministic, uniform certified-window tradeoff on the proved family, explicitly charging \(\Theta(\sqrt\Delta)\). Separate semiprime and randomized extensions.

**9. MAJOR — The generic \(\Delta^{1/3}\) assertion is only an analogy.**

A hidden prime component with a prescribed distribution and bounded transcript coefficients is not arbitrary group-order recovery in an analytic window. Actual class-number distributions supply none of these hypotheses automatically.

**Fix:** call this a candidate lower-bound model. Conditional on a \(\Delta^{1/3}\) finish, the balance \(Q^{1/7}\) is correct; it is neither an achieved algorithm nor an established universal lower bound.

## 3. HW and amortisation

**10. MAJOR — HW’s inequality is correct; its real-coefficient corollary is false.**

The Hankel-rank and singular-value proof works, including properly counted jets. But complex moment errors paired with real coefficient intervals do not have dual norm \(\sum|e_n|\).

Take \(N=W=T=1\), all outside coefficients zero, and
\[
f(z)=a_0+a_1z+a_2z^2,\qquad a_j\in[-A,A].
\]
The one-value rule
\[
\widehat a_1=-\frac{8i}{29}f(i/2)
\]
has worst error
\[
5A/\sqrt{29}<A,
\]
contradicting the claimed requirement \(T\ge2\). Its moment \(\ell^1\)-error is \(35/29>1\), consistent with the true theorem.

**Fix:** use complex coefficient disks, require real moment errors, or weaken the real-cube error threshold by an absolute constant. Count two real measurements in a complex sample explicitly.

**11. MAJOR — HW is not a lower bound for modular coefficients.**

Independent coefficient variations are absent for theta series and modular forms. Contour integration is not itself a finite point-value rule. The theorem does not account for precision or modular relations.

This is an elementary finite Hankel-rank/singular-value consequence, closely related to classical Prony/Kronecker and AAK approximation theory. [Peller, §6](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book33/files/peller.pdf).

**Fix:** restrict the conclusion to the corrected black-box linear information model. Do not infer a lower bound for structured modular coefficient algorithms.

**12. MAJOR — AM proves a density identity, not trial-division complexity.**

The determinant density is correct, including at \(2\). But the identification of complete Hurwitz local mass with that density needs proof. The displayed raw \(L\)-moment factor depends on \((N/\ell)\) even when \(v_\ell(N)=0\).

“Any weight” is too broad. Selecting \(t\equiv0\pmod3\) gives respectively \(6\) and \(12\) trace-zero matrices modulo \(3\) with determinants \(1\) and \(2\), despite equal valuations.

Even a valid Euler-factor identity does not require an aggregate algorithm to evaluate every factor separately.

**Fix:** specify smooth macroscopic averaging and conclude only that *termwise local-factor evaluation performs divisibility tests*.

**13. MAJOR — The Chebyshev algebra is correct, but the residual interpretation omits a term.**

The generating function and sign \(-2pJ_w(\sqrt{p/q})\) are correct. The full formula is
\[
\begin{aligned}
S_w={}&2c_0\sigma(N)-2J_w(N^{-1/2})
-2pJ_w(\sqrt{p/q})\\
&-2\sum_{m\text{ even}}c_mN^{-m/2}
\operatorname{Tr}(T_N|S_{m+2}).
\end{aligned}
\]

Thus E3’s residual after subtracting the \(p\)-term is **not solely cuspidal**. For \(w=1\), it is exactly \(-2\), although no cusp terms occur. For the registered bump, the missing term tends to approximately \(-0.51561658\).

Choosing \(w=U_{10}\) gives the cusp term \(-2N^{-5}\tau(p)\tau(q)\), on the same \(\sqrt N\) scale as the hyperbolic term. “Smaller” is weight-dependent, not a power saving.

**Fix:** retain both divisor terms. Describe \(J_w\) as a transform, not literal evaluation of the bump outside its support. [Trace formula normalization](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/forum-of-mathematics-sigma/article/eichlerselberg-relations-for-singular-moduli/7085064B69DD32BD5B3DE5293ED43EF7).

## 4. Literature, wall table, and verdict

**14. MAJOR — de Haan–Jacobson–Williams is misdescribed.**

Their algorithm **computes** the regulator with unconditional correctness and expected \(D^{1/6+\varepsilon}\) time under GRH. It combines a relation-based producer of a regulator multiple with rigorous verification; §4.3 analyzes the combination.

**Fix:** list this hybrid computation and explain why it lies outside the linear architecture. It is a real sub-\(1/5\) regulator computation. [Author-uploaded paper](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/220577622_A_fast_rigorous_technique_for_computing_the_regulator_of_a_real_quadratic_field).

**15. MAJOR — Correct the speedup qualifications.**

Hiary 2014’s ceiling-product bound is correct; “\(q^{1/3}\) for power-full \(q\)” is not: \(q=p^2\) gives \(q^{1/2}\). Include preprocessing. The paper explicitly permits factoring within its overall bound. [Theorem 1.1](https://arxiv.org/html/1205.4687).

Vishe needs a useful divisor, not necessarily complete factorization, and treats a degree-two twist of conductor roughly \(q^2\). [Theorem 1](https://maths.dur.ac.uk/users/pankaj.vishe/q-aspect.pdf).

Hiary 2018’s preprocessing/divisor costs and use of sieved factorizations are accurately summarized. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1608.06614).

**Fix:** distinguish these requirements instead of grouping them as algorithms that “secretly factor.”

**16. MAJOR — Two wall-table hypotheses are missing.**

Squarefree \(N\) alone does not make \(-4N\) fundamental: \(N=15\) gives primitive conductor \(15\), not \(60\). Require \(N\equiv1\pmod4\), or handle the public \(2\)-part.

The cited genusleak Prop HR requires a useful midpoint and addresses the norm-\(-1\) exception. Its own counterexample \(N=731=17\cdot43\) has norm-\(+1\) unit \(730+27\sqrt{731}\), but its midpoint reflects the public factor \(2\).

**Fix:** restore these qualifications. Replace the termwise “\(\ge\sqrt N\,N^{1/5}\)” by the cost of a specified algorithm, not a lower bound.

**17. MINOR — Several reference checks pass; Srinivasan remains incompletely verified.**

BHK’s certificates require GRH; Proposition 2.3 and conjectural exponent \(2/3\) are correctly reported, with the subexponential bound applying to squarefree inputs. [Original paper](https://arxiv.org/html/1304.6937).

Booker’s abstract supports unconditional termination and GRH \(D^{1/4+\varepsilon}\) complexity, combining algebraic methods and Burgess. [Institutional abstract](https://research-information.bris.ac.uk/en/publications/quadratic-class-numbers-and-character-sums/).

Platt’s CRT/FFT description is accurate. [§4](https://arxiv.org/html/1305.3087). Odlyzko–Schönhage is height amortisation after preprocessing. [Original abstract](https://www.ams.org/tran/1988-309-02/S0002-9947-1988-0961614-2/S0002-9947-1988-0961614-2.pdf).

Lagarias–Odlyzko’s \(x^{1/2+\varepsilon}\) time uses \(x^{1/4+\varepsilon}\) space; the small-space version takes \(x^{3/5+\varepsilon}\). [Original paper](https://www-users.cse.umn.edu/~odlyzko/doc/arch/analytic.pi.of.x.pdf).

Srinivasan’s cited paper concerns **real** quadratic fields. I verified its bibliography but could not retrieve the primary paper to verify theorem numbers or certification. [Author bibliography](https://sites.google.com/site/rsrinivasananitha/home/publications).

**Fix:** preserve this limitation; provide a separate imaginary-class-number/factoring reduction.

**18. MAJOR — The verdict overclaims exhaustiveness.**

Neither theorem classifies every analytic invariant, conductor construction, nonlinear/adaptive estimator, or structured modular coefficient algorithm. Public BHK certificate output does not rule out useful extensions of its twisted-prime-sum machinery.

LIN does not establish a \(K^{-1/2}\) wall throughout the interval up to the AFE length. Finite AFE truncations have tails; the experimental “cliff” is rapid decay and eventual rounding. Correlated class-number errors also need not require separate \(O(1)\) accuracy in every summand.

**Fix:** conclude that LIN supports a restricted linear-estimation barrier. Relational hybrids already escape it; faster finishes and nonlinear or structured methods remain unexcluded. This audit establishes no new faster factoring algorithm.

Validation: **8 tests passed, 1 skipped**. Independent short computations verified the character identity, HW counterexample, and E2/E3 diagnostics. Each computation stayed well below the resource limits.